A C label is scoped to its function. This compiler emitted it globally.

(Named `zza` and not `zz10` on purpose: the series is applied by a shell glob,
which sorts lexicographically, so `EXPERIMENT-zz10-` lands BETWEEN `zz-` and
`zz2-` and is applied ninth from last. It failed to apply and said so, which is
the good outcome, but the next one after `zz9` has to be a letter.)

    :redo     #C goto label

-- FIVE TIMES, in one flat assembler namespace, from the five different
functions in tccpp.c that each write `redo:`. Every `goto redo` in the unit
resolved to whichever definition survived, so control left for an unrelated
function and never came back.

THIS IS WHAT WAS KILLING THE MICRO-C-BUILT tcc. `next()` called
`macro_subst_tok`, then `tok_str_add`, then `begin_macro`; all three returned
correctly; the next statement is `goto redo`, and the process died there. The
last completed marker was the `begin_macro` call, which is why four rounds of
reading pointed at macro expansion. Nothing was wrong with macro expansion.

M2-Planet gets away with a flat namespace because M2-Planet's own source has
globally unique labels -- there is a literal `/* because of how M2-Planet
treats labels */` comment in its switch code saying so. tcc's source does not,
and has no reason to: C says a label is visible only inside the function that
declares it, so five functions may each have a `redo:`, and tcc has 509 gotos.

OUR OWN STAGE 2 HIT THIS AND FIXED IT THE SAME WAY. PROGRESS.md m58 records
qualifying the emitted name with a per-function index and notes explicitly that
this is "stricter than the target requires, at no cost". It turned out to be
exactly what the target requires; the note was written when the target was
M2-Planet's source and not tcc's.

The definition and the reference now both go through one function,
`goto_label_name`, rather than concatenating in two places -- if they ever
disagree the label silently vanishes, which is the failure mode this whole
file keeps producing.

HOW IT WAS FOUND, because it is a better method than the four rounds before it.
Not by reading: by making the program answer a question it could answer.
`begin_macro` was instrumented to report which of three things had gone wrong
-- a null pointer, a store clobbering a neighbouring member, or a bad load --
and it reported NONE of them, then successfully performed the very dereference
that was believed to be faulting. That eliminated the whole function in one
build. What is left after the last statement of a function is the control flow
out of it, and `goto redo` was the only thing there.

REPRODUCER, three lines, both architectures:

    static int a(int n){ int r=0;   redo: r=r+1;  if(r<n) goto redo; return r; }
    static int b(int n){ int r=100; redo: r=r+10; if(r<n) goto redo; return r; }
    int main(void){ return a(3)==3 && b(130)==130 ? 0 : 1; }

`a(3)` returned 1. Case 52.

MEASURED, both architectures, patched tables:

    amd64     pass 51  fail 0
    aarch64   pass 51  fail 0
    regression.sh      0 regressions

AND tcc MOVES A LONG WAY. It now gets out of macro expansion entirely and into
compiling the input file, reporting real diagnostics with file context:

    In file included from seven.c:0:
    <command line>:27: warning: integer constant overflow

It still faults, later and elsewhere. The `integer constant overflow` warning
is new information and is not yet explained -- micro-c's `int` is eight bytes,
so a predef constant that fits for gcc need not fit here; that is the next
thing to look at, and it is a warning rather than the fault.

